==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|recovre}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*keh₂p-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|recoveren}}, {{m|enm|rekeveren}}, from {{der|en|xno|recoverer}} and {{der|en|fro|recovrer}}, from {{der|en|la|recupero|recuperāre}}, alternative form of {{m|la|recipero|reciperāre}}. {{doublet|en|recuperate}}.

The noun is from {{inh|en|enm|recover}}, from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=3600841799}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|en|UK}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈkʌvə/|a=RP,SSB}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈkʊvə/|a=Northern England,Midlands}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈkəvə/|a=Wales}}
** {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈkʌvəɾ/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈkʌvɚ/|/ɹəˈkʌvɚ/|a=GA}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-recover.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈkɐvə/|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹəˈkɐvə/|a=NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌvə(ɹ)|s=3}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[get back]], to [[regain]] (a physical thing; in astronomy and navigation, sight of a thing or a signal).
#: {{ux|en|After days of inquiries, he finally '''recovered''' his lost wallet.}}
#: {{ux|en|For days telescopes surveyed the skies to '''recover''' the small asteroid.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|1 Samuel|30|18|page=405|column=1|text=And Dauid '''recouered''' all that the Amalekites had caried away: and Dauid reſcued his two wiues.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=22|passage=Not unnaturally, &ldquo;Auntie&rdquo; took this communication in bad part. Thus outraged, she showed herself to be a bold as well as a furious virago. Next day she found her way to their lodgings and tried to '''recover''' her ward by the hair of the head.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1979-12-16|author={{w|Chiang Ching-kuo|Ching-kuo Chiang}}|title=Chairman Chiang Ching-kuo's Opening Address to the KMT Plenum|journal=zh:自由中國週報<t:Free China Weekly>|url=https://archive.org/details/sim_free-china-weekly_1979-12-16_20_49/|volume=XX|issue=49|location=[[Taipei]]|issn=0016-0318|oclc=1786626|page=3|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_free-china-weekly_1979-12-16_20_49/page/n2/|column=1
|text=All the comrades of the Party must strive with maximum courage and unswerving determination, together with our compatriots of the whole country, to carry out our historic task of '''recovering''' the mainland and reshaping the Chinese nation.}}
#* {{see more citations|en}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[salvage]], to [[extricate]], to [[rescue]] (a thing or person).
#: {{ux|en|They '''recovered''' three of the explorers very much alive, then another, wracked with severe hypothermia, who was taken to hospital.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[replenish]] to, [[resume]] (a good state of mind or body).
#: {{ux|en|At the top of the hill I asked to stop for a few minutes to '''recover''' my strength.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Dana Santas|title=Recovering from surgery or stress? Yoga can help|url=https://www.cnn.com/2017/02/17/health/yoga-surgery-recovery-restorative-fitness-santas|date=2017-02-17|passage=Whether you need to '''recover''' from a stressful day, a strenuous workout or even an injury or surgery, restorative yoga may be just the thing for you.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250905052936/https://www.cnn.com/2017/02/17/health/yoga-surgery-recovery-restorative-fitness-santas/|archivedate=5 September 2025}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|legal}} To obtain a positive judgement; to win in a lawsuit.
#: {{ux|en|The plaintiff '''recovered''' in his suit, being awarded declaratory relief and a clearing of his name.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|law}} To gain as compensation or reparation, usually by formal legal process.
#: {{syn|en|recoup}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''recover''' damages in trespass; to '''recover''' debt and costs in a legal action or that is owing}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''recover''' lands in [[ejectment]] or [[common recovery]]}}
#: {{collocation|en|'''recover''' one's investment}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[reach]] (a place), arrive at.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Two Gentlemen of Verona|V|i|passage=The forest is not three leagues off; / If we '''recover''' that, we're sure enough.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Holy Warre|chapter=[[w:Theobald I of Navarre|Theobald King of Navarre]] Maketh an Unsuccessfull Voyage into Palestine|page=179|passage=But with much ado the Christians '''recovered''' to Antioch, having ſcarce a third part of them left, their horſes all dead, and themſelves ſcarce mounted on their legs, miſerably weak; as what the mercy of ſword, plague, and famine had pleaſed to ſpare.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1646|author=w:John Hales|title=Golden Remains of the Ever Memorable Mr. John Hales
|passage=Except he could '''recover''' one of the Cities of Refuge he was to die.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[restore]] to good health, consciousness, life etc.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|II|ii|passage=The wine in my bottle will '''recover''' him.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|2 Timothy|2|26|page=1454|column=2|text=And that they may '''recouer''' themſelues out of the ſnare of the deuill, who are taken captiue by him at his will.}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|passage=Cnelius a physician{{...}}gave him a clyster, by which he was speedily '''recovered'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage=The fever must have run its course by now, and if he is about to die I will '''recover''' him.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To make good by reparation; to make up for; to retrieve; to repair the loss or injury of.
#: {{ux|en|to '''recover''' lost time}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1729|author={{w|John Rogers (priest)|John Rogers}}|title=The Difficulties of Obtaining Salvation
|passage=Even good men have {{...}}many failings and lapses to lament and '''recover'''.}}
#* '''21 May, 1665''', {{w|Abraham Cowley}}, ''letter to Dr. Thomas Sprat''
#* {{RQ:Defoe Crusoe|passage=when I had '''recovered''' a little, Friday (said I) God will at last punish him severely}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[regain]] one's composure, [[balance]] etc.
#: {{ux|en|Spinning round, he caught a stone with his ankle, but '''recovered''' quickly before turning to face me.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=19|passage=Meanwhile Nanny Broome was '''recovering''' from her initial panic and seemed anxious to make up for any kudos she might have lost, by exerting her personality to the utmost. She took the policeman's helmet and placed it on a chair, and unfolded his tunic to shake it and fold it up again for him.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|followed by "from" to show what caused the bad feeling}} To [[get better]], to regain health or prosperity.
#: {{ux|en|I was hurt, but I knew I’d '''recover''', given time.}}
#: {{ux|en|Without calling in Business Recovery experts, the company saw trade and investor confidence '''recover''' sharply in the wake of the crisis.}}
#: {{ux|en|I lost out in the deal, but I quickly '''recovered''' financially}}
#: {{ux|en|It takes time and good health to '''recover''' from injury, surgery, a bereavement and emotional turmoil}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic|without "from"}} To recover from.
#: {{ux|en|To the end of his days, he never fully '''recovered''' his daughter's death.}}

